WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:03.560
- [Instructor] Let's say that
we have the function g of x,

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:08.560
and it is equal to the
definite integral from 19 to x

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.370
of the cube root of t dt.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:14.500
And what I'm curious about finding

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:19.500
or trying to figure out
is, what is g prime of 27?

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:20.580
What is that equal to?

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.070
Pause this video and
try to think about it,

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:23.910
and I'll give you a little bit of a hint.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:28.400
Think about the second
fundamental theorem of calculus.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.550
All right, now let's
work on this together.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.670
So we wanna figure out what g prime,

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.340
we could try to figure
out what g prime of x is,

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:35.900
and then evaluate that at 27,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.850
and the best way that I
can think about doing that

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:41.090
is by taking the derivative of
both sides of this equation.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:44.150
So let's take the derivative
of both sides of that equation.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.150
So the left-hand side,
we'll take the derivative

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:47.900
with respect to x of g of x,

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.430
and the right-hand side, the
derivative with respect to x

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:53.550
of all of this business.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.570
Now, the left-hand side is
pretty straight forward.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:57.640
The derivative with
respect to x of g of x,

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.270
that's just going to be g prime of x,

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:04.400
but what is the right-hand
side going to be equal to?

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.610
Well, that's where the
second fundamental theorem

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.520
of calculus is useful.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:09.640
I'll write it right over here.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:14.233
Second fundamental, I'll
abbreviate a little bit,

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.760
theorem of calculus.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.710
It tells us, let's say we have
some function capital F of x,

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:28.930
and it's equal to the
definite integral from a,

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:33.930
sum constant a to x of
lowercase f of t dt.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.940
The second fundamental
theorem of calculus tells us

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:41.180
that if our lowercase f,

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:46.180
if lowercase f is continuous
on the interval from a to x,

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:48.560
so I'll write it this way,

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.023
on the closed interval from a to x,

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:57.660
then the derivative of our capital f of x,

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.920
so capital F prime of x
is just going to be equal

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.090
to our inner function f
evaluated at x instead of t

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.020
is going to become lowercase f of x.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.390
Now, I know when you first saw this,

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:12.207
you thought that, "Hey, this
might be some cryptic thing

00:02:12.207 --> 00:02:14.240
"that you might not use too often."

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.650
Well, we're gonna see that
it's actually very, very useful

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.300
and even in the future, and
some of you might already know,

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.730
there's multiple ways to try

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:22.430
to think about a definite
integral like this,

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.030
and you'll learn it in the future.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.090
But this can be extremely simplifying,

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:30.430
especially if you have a hairy
definite integral like this,

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:32.760
and so this just tells us,
hey, look, the derivative

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.020
with respect to x of all of this business,

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:37.610
first we have to check
that our inner function,

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.120
which would be analogous
to our lowercase f here,

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:44.620
is this continuous on the
interval from 19 to x?

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.800
Well, no matter what x is,

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.180
this is going to be
continuous over that interval,

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.290
because this is continuous for all x's,

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:57.110
and so we meet this first
condition or our major condition,

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.850
and so then we can just say, all right,

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:01.170
then the derivative of all of this

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:05.020
is just going to be this inner
function replacing t with x.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:06.490
So we're going to get the cube root,

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:07.800
instead of the cube root of t,

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.800
you're gonna get the cube root of x.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.620
And so we can go back to
our original question,

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:16.230
what is g prime of 27
going to be equal to?

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:19.490
Well, it's going to be equal
to the cube root of 27,

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:22.563
which is of course equal
to three, and we're done.

